  "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =18{x}^{2}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =36x+\\frac{36}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36x+\\frac{36}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 36(\\frac{{x}^{5}+1}{{x}^{4}}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{5}+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{\u2033}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(-2) & =36(-2)+\\frac{36}{(-2)^{4}}=-\\frac{279}{4}<0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(1) & =36(1)+\\frac{36}{(1)^{4}}=72>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection.",
